<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hà Tiên</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ha Tien, Peam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hà Tiên is a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, near the Cambodian border. Originally the Cambodian town of Peam, it was developed in the late 17th century by the Chinese exile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-cuu">Mạc Cửu</a> into a trading port, which he placed under Vietnamese Nguyễn protection around 1708. It became a hereditary principality of the Mạc family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ha-tien.jpg" alt="Hà Tiên" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trương Thành Ý · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quang_truong_ha_tien.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
